1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,309
At the very bottom of the land in Ayodhya, a handful of coal was found.

2
00:00:04,309 --> 00:00:07,757
That coal gave a date: 910 BCE.

3
00:00:07,757 --> 00:00:12,497
That means even 910 years before Christ, people lived here,

4
00:00:12,497 --> 00:00:16,375
and lit cooking fires. This is not a matter of some story,

5
00:00:16,375 --> 00:00:18,530
this is a lab report.

6
00:00:18,530 --> 00:00:22,409
This report is from the Archaeological Survey of India, that is, ASI.

7
00:00:22,409 --> 00:00:24,736
ASI is the country's government body

8
00:00:24,736 --> 00:00:28,226
that digs and looks after old sites.

9
00:00:28,226 --> 00:00:31,329
But that report did not have only coal.

10
00:00:31,329 --> 00:00:34,820
Even today, scholars debate some of the things in it.

11
00:00:34,820 --> 00:00:38,150
The foundation of a large building was also found under the ground.

12
00:00:38,150 --> 00:00:41,479
Some said this was a huge building with pillars.

13
00:00:41,479 --> 00:00:42,478
Some said,

14
00:00:42,478 --> 00:00:45,475
these are pieces of floors from different periods.

15
00:00:45,475 --> 00:00:47,472
There are educated people on both sides,

16
00:00:47,472 --> 00:00:49,470
and both have arguments.

17
00:00:49,470 --> 00:00:52,919
We will go with the evidence, and we begin with the scriptures.

18
00:00:52,919 --> 00:00:56,369
First let us see how the old scriptures show Ayodhya.

19
00:00:56,369 --> 00:00:58,877
Because the meaning of the excavation is understood only then.

20
00:00:58,877 --> 00:01:02,640
When you know what people were looking for under the ground.

21
00:01:02,640 --> 00:01:05,589
The first part of Valmiki's Ramayana is the Balakanda.

22
00:01:05,589 --> 00:01:10,224
In it, Ayodhya is a fortified capital, on the bank of the Saryu river.

23
00:01:10,224 --> 00:01:14,437
A safe and beautiful city, surrounded by strong walls.

24
00:01:14,437 --> 00:01:18,650
According to the Ramayana, this city was settled by Manu.

25
00:01:18,650 --> 00:01:19,881
And its size?

26
00:01:19,881 --> 00:01:23,576
According to the Balakanda, Ayodhya was 12 yojana long.

27
00:01:23,576 --> 00:01:27,270
Yojana is a unit for measuring distance in ancient times.

28
00:01:27,270 --> 00:01:31,785
Scholars calculate it differently in today's kilometres.

29
00:01:31,785 --> 00:01:36,300
But the message is clear, this is a picture of a huge city.

30
00:01:36,300 --> 00:01:40,263
Then comes the Skanda Purana, one part of which is the Ayodhya Mahatmya.

31
00:01:40,263 --> 00:01:44,585
A Mahatmya is a text that describes the glory of a holy place and gives its map.

32
00:01:44,585 --> 00:01:48,188
In it, the whole geography of Ayodhya's sacred places is written.

33
00:01:48,188 --> 00:01:52,150
Which place is where, which ashram is in which direction.

34
00:01:52,150 --> 00:01:56,473
In this same text, the location of the Janmasthan is also given by directions.

35
00:01:56,473 --> 00:02:00,435
It is east of Vighneshwar, and north of Vashishtha.

36
00:02:00,435 --> 00:02:03,677
And also west of the ashram of sage Lomash.

37
00:02:03,677 --> 00:02:06,919
So the text gives a kind of old map,

38
00:02:06,919 --> 00:02:08,360
without any map.

39
00:02:08,360 --> 00:02:12,218
Besides the scriptures, there are also stories told by word of mouth.

40
00:02:12,218 --> 00:02:16,933
One such story is recorded in the old District Gazetteer of Faizabad.

41
00:02:16,933 --> 00:02:19,077
A gazetteer is a government book

42
00:02:19,077 --> 00:02:22,935
that records the history and present condition of a district.

43
00:02:22,935 --> 00:02:27,650
The story goes that King Vikramaditya resettled Ayodhya.

44
00:02:27,650 --> 00:02:31,833
And he had a magnificent temple built, standing on 84 pillars.

45
00:02:31,833 --> 00:02:36,017
The pillars were of black stone, which people called kasauti.

46
00:02:36,017 --> 00:02:38,108
Please note, this is a legend,

47
00:02:38,108 --> 00:02:41,037
with no proof from any ancient text or excavation.

48
00:02:41,037 --> 00:02:45,220
But such stories have always kept people's curiosity alive.

49
00:02:45,220 --> 00:02:50,443
Now the story leaves the books and goes down into the soil.

50
00:02:50,443 --> 00:02:56,188
The year is 2003, and a big excavation begins in Ayodhya.

51
00:02:56,188 --> 00:03:01,934
Many people had their eyes on this excavation. Let's see

52
00:03:01,934 --> 00:03:05,590
what the ASI did, and how they did it.

53
00:03:05,590 --> 00:03:09,649
From March to August, the ASI team worked here.

54
00:03:09,649 --> 00:03:12,970
This excavation was not being done on their own wish.

55
00:03:12,970 --> 00:03:17,030
The Lucknow bench of the Allahabad High Court had ordered it.

56
00:03:17,030 --> 00:03:20,720
So every step had to be taken carefully, and in writing.

57
00:03:20,720 --> 00:03:24,064
Before the excavation, another investigation had been done,

58
00:03:24,064 --> 00:03:28,662
a Ground Penetrating Radar survey. That means sending radar into the ground

59
00:03:28,662 --> 00:03:31,588
to get a picture of hidden things without digging.

60
00:03:31,588 --> 00:03:36,186
The radar showed some strange marks in a few places, called anomalies.

61
00:03:36,186 --> 00:03:39,530
The ASI had to check the truth behind these marks.

62
00:03:39,530 --> 00:03:42,468
For this, the ASI dug 82 trenches.

63
00:03:42,468 --> 00:03:44,987
A trench is a square pit for excavation,

64
00:03:44,987 --> 00:03:48,345
where the soil is removed layer by layer.

65
00:03:48,345 --> 00:03:52,542
Each trench was like a window to peek into the past.

66
00:03:52,542 --> 00:03:55,900
And every window showed something.

67
00:03:55,900 --> 00:03:59,598
In an excavation, the layers of soil are the most important evidence.

68
00:03:59,598 --> 00:04:00,707
A layer is called a stratum,

69
00:04:00,707 --> 00:04:04,405
the level of soil that settled during one period of time.

70
00:04:04,405 --> 00:04:07,363
The oldest layer is at the bottom, and the newer ones on top.

71
00:04:07,363 --> 00:04:11,430
Like newspapers stacked one on another, the one at the bottom is the oldest.

72
00:04:11,430 --> 00:04:13,882
The team had archaeologists, laborers,

73
00:04:13,882 --> 00:04:18,376
and supervising officers too. Baskets of soil were sifted,

74
00:04:18,376 --> 00:04:22,462
and the pieces found were kept separately. Every item was photographed,

75
00:04:22,462 --> 00:04:24,505
and its map and depth were recorded.

76
00:04:24,505 --> 00:04:29,000
Because every page of this excavation was to be examined later.

77
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:32,715
Now we come to the real question: what did the soil tell us?

78
00:04:32,715 --> 00:04:35,688
Let's begin with the lowest layer.

79
00:04:35,688 --> 00:04:39,775
Because that is the one that tells when humans first came here.

80
00:04:39,775 --> 00:04:43,490
The story of an excavation is always read from the bottom up.

81
00:04:43,490 --> 00:04:47,603
ASI named the oldest layer Period I.

82
00:04:47,603 --> 00:04:51,716
This layer yielded distinctive pottery, Northern Black Polished Ware.

83
00:04:51,716 --> 00:04:53,772
That is, black, shiny, thin pots,

84
00:04:53,772 --> 00:04:57,885
which are considered a hallmark of the cities of ancient India.

85
00:04:57,885 --> 00:05:02,820
According to ASI, these people were the first settlers of this place.

86
00:05:02,820 --> 00:05:05,773
Clay figurines were also found in this same layer,

87
00:05:05,773 --> 00:05:07,249
which are called terracotta.

88
00:05:07,249 --> 00:05:10,202
That is, small figures made of baked clay.

89
00:05:10,202 --> 00:05:12,417
Glass beads were found along with them,

90
00:05:12,417 --> 00:05:15,001
which were probably strung into jewellery.

91
00:05:15,001 --> 00:05:18,323
Everything shows that there was a settlement here,

92
00:05:18,323 --> 00:05:19,430
not just a camp.

93
00:05:19,430 --> 00:05:24,094
And then a round seal was found, with something written on it.

94
00:05:24,094 --> 00:05:26,215
The writing was in Ashokan Brahmi.

95
00:05:26,215 --> 00:05:32,151
Brahmi is one of the oldest readable scripts of India.

96
00:05:32,151 --> 00:05:36,392
The same script that is found on the rock edicts of Emperor Ashok.

97
00:05:36,392 --> 00:05:39,360
One small seal, but a big question,

98
00:05:39,360 --> 00:05:41,480
who might have used it?

99
00:05:41,480 --> 00:05:45,754
Now about the charcoal, with which we started the video.

100
00:05:45,754 --> 00:05:49,639
Pieces of charcoal were taken out from the Period I layer.

101
00:05:49,639 --> 00:05:51,582
Then they were radiocarbon dated.

102
00:05:51,582 --> 00:05:54,302
Radiocarbon dating means a scientific way to calculate

103
00:05:54,302 --> 00:05:57,022
how old any organic material is.

104
00:05:57,022 --> 00:06:00,130
Organic means something that was once alive, like wood.

105
00:06:00,130 --> 00:06:02,420
The result came out around 910 BCE.

106
00:06:02,420 --> 00:06:05,854
Another sample also came out close to the same period.

107
00:06:05,854 --> 00:06:09,289
Meaning, in very ancient times, even before Ashok,

108
00:06:09,289 --> 00:06:10,816
people lived here.

109
00:06:10,816 --> 00:06:13,869
The report itself admits that in such dates,

110
00:06:13,869 --> 00:06:16,540
there is some room for error.

111
00:06:16,540 --> 00:06:20,613
Now let's move to the upper layers, to the medieval period.

112
00:06:20,613 --> 00:06:24,685
The medieval period means the long era between ancient and modern times.

113
00:06:24,685 --> 00:06:27,128
Here ASI found something

114
00:06:27,128 --> 00:06:29,572
that drew the most discussion.

115
00:06:29,572 --> 00:06:32,830
Traces of a large structure under the ground.

116
00:06:32,830 --> 00:06:36,867
According to ASI, this structure was about 50 metres long.

117
00:06:36,867 --> 00:06:39,289
Its orientation was north to south.

118
00:06:39,289 --> 00:06:42,519
The report described it as early medieval. Meaning,

119
00:06:42,519 --> 00:06:46,960
the foundation of some very big structure that once stood here.

120
00:06:46,960 --> 00:06:51,897
The report also records the bases of several pillars connected with this structure.

121
00:06:51,897 --> 00:06:56,075
The base of a pillar, meaning the platform on which the pillar stands.

122
00:06:56,075 --> 00:06:57,594
The ASI said,

123
00:06:57,594 --> 00:07:01,012
these bases belonged to a big hall with many pillars.

124
00:07:01,012 --> 00:07:04,810
Meaning, there was once a huge building here, resting on pillars.

125
00:07:04,810 --> 00:07:08,746
From here the story does not stay simple. The ASI report came out,

126
00:07:08,746 --> 00:07:11,107
and with it came questions.

127
00:07:11,107 --> 00:07:14,649
Some independent archaeologists read the report carefully.

128
00:07:14,649 --> 00:07:18,585
Independent means people who were not part of this excavation team.

129
00:07:18,585 --> 00:07:21,340
And they put forward a different picture.

130
00:07:21,340 --> 00:07:24,402
They said that what has been called pillar bases

131
00:07:24,402 --> 00:07:26,443
are actually something else.

132
00:07:26,443 --> 00:07:29,845
According to them, these were pieces of a brick floor.

133
00:07:29,845 --> 00:07:32,567
And these pieces are not from the same period,

134
00:07:32,567 --> 00:07:35,969
they are from different eras. Meaning, layers from different times

135
00:07:35,969 --> 00:07:37,670
were showing up together.

136
00:07:37,670 --> 00:07:39,635
This argument has big implications.

137
00:07:39,635 --> 00:07:41,928
If the bases are from different eras,

138
00:07:41,928 --> 00:07:44,548
then they cannot belong to one hall.

139
00:07:44,548 --> 00:07:46,840
Then they are not load-bearing pillars,

140
00:07:46,840 --> 00:07:48,478
just parts of a floor.

141
00:07:48,478 --> 00:07:52,080
In this view, a huge pillared building is not proven.

142
00:07:52,080 --> 00:07:54,947
On the other side, the ASI stood by its report.

143
00:07:54,947 --> 00:07:58,890
According to it, the length of the foundation, its direction and the remaining structure

144
00:07:58,890 --> 00:08:01,757
point toward a large construction.

145
00:08:01,757 --> 00:08:03,549
There are trained archaeologists on both sides.

146
00:08:03,549 --> 00:08:07,850
And both are reading the same soil differently.

147
00:08:07,850 --> 00:08:11,058
This often happens in archaeology.

148
00:08:11,058 --> 00:08:16,940
Archaeology means the science of reading the past from evidence buried in the soil.

149
00:08:16,940 --> 00:08:21,217
The soil does not speak by itself, it has to be read.

150
00:08:21,217 --> 00:08:26,029
A piece of brick is a base for one person,

151
00:08:26,029 --> 00:08:30,841
a floor for another. That is why even after the report,

152
00:08:30,841 --> 00:08:32,980
the debate does not end.

153
00:08:32,980 --> 00:08:36,456
Now the part that often gets left out of the news.

154
00:08:36,456 --> 00:08:39,931
The question to which even the excavation does not have a clear answer.

155
00:08:39,931 --> 00:08:43,407
In an honest story, 'don't know' is also an answer.

156
00:08:43,407 --> 00:08:47,230
And going with the evidence means saying that plainly too.

157
00:08:47,230 --> 00:08:50,165
Suppose there was a large medieval building there.

158
00:08:50,165 --> 00:08:53,100
Then the question arises, how did it come to an end?

159
00:08:53,100 --> 00:08:56,403
Did it slowly fall into ruins over time?

160
00:08:56,403 --> 00:08:58,971
Or was it deliberately brought down?

161
00:08:58,971 --> 00:09:02,640
The excavation evidence doesn't settle this question clearly.

162
00:09:02,640 --> 00:09:05,694
Between that building and the later construction,

163
00:09:05,694 --> 00:09:09,511
there is a gap of several centuries. In such a long time, rain,

164
00:09:09,511 --> 00:09:12,564
floods and time, all do their work.

165
00:09:12,564 --> 00:09:15,618
The date of something falling into the soil is not written anywhere.

166
00:09:15,618 --> 00:09:18,290
That's why this question is still open today.

167
00:09:18,290 --> 00:09:22,253
There are more questions left. How big was the oldest settlement?

168
00:09:22,253 --> 00:09:25,495
Whose seal was it, and what did its owner do?

169
00:09:25,495 --> 00:09:29,097
Where does the Ayodhya of the scriptures meet the Ayodhya of the soil,

170
00:09:29,097 --> 00:09:29,818
and where do they differ?

171
00:09:29,818 --> 00:09:33,420
Only further research can shed light on these.

172
00:09:33,420 --> 00:09:37,190
If you visit Ayodhya today, start at the ghats of the Saryu.

173
00:09:37,190 --> 00:09:39,588
The same river that is mentioned in the Balakanda.

174
00:09:39,588 --> 00:09:43,015
In the evening aarti, the ghats fill up with lamps.

175
00:09:43,015 --> 00:09:45,414
The river of the scriptures and today's river

176
00:09:45,414 --> 00:09:47,470
flow under the same name.

177
00:09:47,470 --> 00:09:51,417
Walk through the lanes of the old city, see the temples and ghats.

178
00:09:51,417 --> 00:09:55,758
The place where the excavation was done is a place of worship today.

179
00:09:55,758 --> 00:09:57,731
Devotees come there every day.

180
00:09:57,731 --> 00:10:00,493
And those who want to read the evidence themselves,

181
00:10:00,493 --> 00:10:04,440
can start with the ASI website, asi.nic.in.

182
00:10:04,440 --> 00:10:08,465
If you see black, shiny NBPW pottery in a museum,

183
00:10:08,465 --> 00:10:10,880
stop. Look at it carefully.

184
00:10:10,880 --> 00:10:15,710
These are the very pots that were found in the oldest layer of Ayodhya.

185
00:10:15,710 --> 00:10:18,930
A small piece, and a very long story.

186
00:10:18,930 --> 00:10:21,698
So what does it all add up to? The scriptures show a grand,

187
00:10:21,698 --> 00:10:23,674
fortified Ayodhya.

188
00:10:23,674 --> 00:10:27,628
The soil says that people had settled here long ago,

189
00:10:27,628 --> 00:10:30,000
and left behind pots, beads and seals.

190
00:10:30,000 --> 00:10:32,372
On one big building in between,

191
00:10:32,372 --> 00:10:35,140
scholars still hold two opinions.

192
00:10:35,140 --> 00:10:37,810
And the biggest question is this.

193
00:10:37,810 --> 00:10:42,705
Will the scriptures and the soil ever tell one single story completely?

194
00:10:42,705 --> 00:10:47,600
Maybe new methods and new research will open up more layers. Until then,

195
00:10:47,600 --> 00:10:52,050
call what is certain certain, and what is open,

196
00:10:52,050 --> 00:10:53,830
let it stay open.

197
00:10:53,830 --> 00:10:57,509
Next time we'll head towards the sea, to Dwarka.

198
00:10:57,509 --> 00:11:02,109
They say Krishna's city sank into the water.

199
00:11:02,109 --> 00:11:06,708
What did the divers see there, and what does science say?

200
00:11:06,708 --> 00:11:09,468
But before that, tell me in the comments.

201
00:11:09,468 --> 00:11:14,527
What surprised you the most: the coal from 910 BCE,

202
00:11:14,527 --> 00:11:16,367
or the seal with the Brahmi script?
